# USC-II skeletal reduction: accuracy and size trade-off

## Result and recommendation

Three new skeletal mechanisms reduce the supplied **111-species, 784-reaction** parent. The **58-species / 420-reaction model is the recommended general option within the tested domain**: it removes 47.7% of species and 46.4% of reactions while retaining both synthetic and external-case parent fidelity. The 53- and 49-species options offer greater compression for the tested air mixtures, with an explicit failure of their flame-fidelity targets in oxygen-diluted, rich acetylene.

| Submitted mechanism | Species / reaction reduction | Max ignition error to parent | Max flame-speed error to parent |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| [58 species / 420 reactions](mechanisms/usc2_58sp_420rxn.yaml) | 47.7% / 46.4% | 4.04% | 1.17% |
| [53 species / 374 reactions](mechanisms/usc2_53sp_374rxn.yaml) | 52.3% / 52.3% | 3.50% | 4.91% |
| [49 species / 330 reactions](mechanisms/usc2_49sp_330rxn.yaml) | 55.9% / 57.9% | 8.14% | 9.50% |

These maxima cover **234 ignition and 45 flame conditions per mechanism**, including 90 conditions reserved before design. All 1,116 parent/candidate synthetic simulations succeeded and all ignition cases ignited. Relative errors are `prediction / reference - 1`; displayed maxima use absolute values. These are finite-case bounds, not guarantees across a continuous parameter range.

External comparison is a separate result: the recommended mechanism has **28.77% mean absolute ignition error** over 26 observed conditions and **6.70% mean absolute flame-speed error** over 24 targets. Parent errors are 28.97% and 6.77%. Reduction preserves the parent's useful behavior and substantial existing ignition discrepancies; it does not establish uniformly accurate experimental prediction.

