## 1. Intended domain and evidence coverage

The application domain is high-temperature oxidation of H2, 30/70 molar H2/CO, CH4, 70/30 molar CH4/H2, C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, C3H6 and C3H8. This tests hydrogen, CO chemistry, alkane, alkene and alkyne families and two blends. It deliberately narrows USC-II's original scope: C4 fuels, aromatic fuels, oxygenated fuels, low-temperature/peroxy ignition, soot and pollutant yields are omitted. Higher-carbon intermediates remain essential even for these smaller fuels.

Park et al. (2016), *Reaction models*, describes USC-II as 111 species/784 reactions for H2/CO and C1–C4 high-temperature chemistry, and summarizes its ignition, flame and species-profile validation, including benzene/toluene. Shao et al. (2018) gives the Wang et al. (2007) USC-Mech-II citation and an independent shock-tube assessment. Original USC-II website/mirror retrieval attempts failed; this report does not claim to have recovered the full original validation archive. Park's optimized kinetic model is **not** used here.

### Synthetic reference design

All synthetic mixtures use dry air, O2:N2 = 1:3.76. Temperature values below are initial reactor or unburned flame temperatures. One atmosphere is 101325 Pa.

| Role | Observable | Conditions, crossed over all nine fuels/blends | Count |
|---|---|---|---:|
| Development | Adiabatic constant-volume ignition, max dT/dt | 1100/1400/1700 K; 1/10 atm; phi 0.6/1/1.5 | 162 |
| Development | Unstretched freely propagating flame speed | 300 K; 1 atm; phi 0.7/1/1.4 | 27 |
| Reserved confirmation | Ignition | 1200/1550 K; 3/20 atm; phi 0.8/1.2 | 72 |
| Reserved confirmation | Flame speed | 400 K; 3 atm; phi 0.85/1.15 | 18 |

Lean/rich mixtures and multiple pressures probe radical branching, termination and thermal propagation. Confirmation includes new temperatures, mixtures and pressures, including a pressure extension. This is a designed coverage grid, not a statistical sample of an operating population. It omits extinction, strain-dependent flames, turbulent combustion, engine heat loss, facility histories, and experimental ignition for ethane, acetylene and the exact synthetic H2/CO and CH4/H2 blends. Pure-H2 external flame measurements were not included; light-fuel flame evidence is H2/CO.

Before screening, maximum parent-fidelity tolerances were set to **5% ignition / 3% flame** (tight), **10% / 5%** (main), and **20% / 10%** (exploratory compression). These engineering screening choices are smaller than many observed parent/experiment discrepancies, yet permit meaningful compression. They are not measurement uncertainties. No tolerance was relaxed after a candidate failed.

## 2. Method and how evidence changed it

All submitted YAML files were **designed in this run** by species and reaction deletion from `inputs/parent.yaml`; no existing reduced mechanism was downloaded and relabeled. Parent SHA-256: `eefd846bf67253ecf8a35c99bf2387b9a38b3f69a94640c013d99b0f16277aee`. No rate, thermodynamic or transport parameters were fitted.

1. A carbon-number cutoff supplied an inspectable baseline. Keeping only C≤3 species gave 55 species/395 reactions and a **590.17%** maximum ignition error despite only 2.63% flame error. It was rejected.
2. Parent development trajectories and flame profiles supplied **22,453 sampled states** for directed relation graph with error propagation (DRGEP). The direct coefficient is `r_AB = abs(sum_j nu_Aj q_j delta_Bj) / max(P_A,C_A)`, using net reaction contributions; path importance is the maximum product of coefficients along directed paths. State-wise maxima form the species ranking.
3. Initial targets included radicals and CO even in pure-H2 cases. Numerically tiny carbon pathways then received misleading importance. Targets were changed to each case's actual fuel species and O2; all domain fuels, AR/N2, O2, H2O, CO2 and H/O/OH/HO2/H2O2 remained protected independently of graph rank.
4. Complete macroscopic tests showed that graph threshold alone was insufficient. Threshold 0.1 retained 93 species/679 reactions with 2.82% ignition and 0.59% flame error; 0.3 gave 86/622 and 20.16%/2.12%; thresholds 0.5–0.9 caused 471–933% maximum ignition errors. This prompted initial deletion sensitivity following the established DRGEP-plus-sensitivity approach described by pyMARS.
5. Each of 75 unprotected species was individually deleted from the 93-species mechanism and tested on all 162 development ignition cases plus nine stoichiometric flames. Deletions were ranked by incremental change from that 93-species baseline, `max(delta_ignition/0.10, delta_flame/0.05)`. Ranking by absolute parent error can mask small deletion effects, so that initial score was not used for joint selection. Every joint candidate was then tested on all 189 development conditions.
6. Cumulative deletion of 35, 40 and 44 ranked species produced the submitted 58/420, 53/374 and 49/330 models. All final development flames were recomputed on finer grids. Candidates and hashes were frozen in `results/candidate_freeze.json` before any confirmation or external simulation result was read. External findings changed the recommendation and scope, not the frozen chemistry.

### Alternative designs and rejected attempts

| Development candidate | Species/reactions | Max ignition / flame error | Interpretation |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| C≤3 structural cutoff | 55/395 | 590.17% / 2.63% | Reject: ignition chemistry lost |
| Sensitivity cumulative 25 deletions | 68/512 | 2.97% / 0.90% | Accurate, larger |
| 30 deletions | 63/450 | 2.52% / 0.65% | Accurate, larger |
| 45 deletions | 48/324 | 7.24% / 11.32% | Reject: exceeds exploratory flame limit |
| Pathway design retaining selected C4 chemistry | 61/430 | 8.83% / 0.95% | Viable main-domain alternative |
| Same plus C4H10 | 62/432 | 4.37% / 1.03% | Larger tight alternative; inherited CH2OCH thermo jump |

These exploratory flame figures use the initial grid, so their sub-percent differences should not be overinterpreted. Final submitted figures use the common finer grid. Intermediate 51/366 and 50/343 candidates gave fine-grid development ignition/flame errors of 6.80%/5.15% and 7.11%/9.43%; neither offered a distinct tolerance tier over the selected models. Errors are nonmonotonic: a smaller model can benefit from cancellation without implying a better physical description.

Flux inspection explains why a fuel carbon-number cutoff was unsafe. In an acetylene induction state near 1150 K, omitted `C2H + C2H2 <=> C4H2 + H` and `C4H2 + OH <=> H + H2C4O` contributed about 0.0526 and 0.0251 kmol m^-3 s^-1 of H production. A separate propylene pathway test implicated `CH3 + aC3H5 (+M) <=> C4H81 (+M)` termination; adding C4H81 reduced an approximately 35% ignition error to about 9%. These are specific-state observations, not global causal proofs. Raw diagnoses and alternative files remain in `results/` and `mechanisms/`.

The custom DRGEP matrix agreed with the inspected pyMARS implementation at five USC-II states within 7e-15; path coefficients agreed with SciPy negative-log Dijkstra within 3e-17, and a hand-checkable graph passed. The numerical driver also received an independent, read-only researcher code review with no concrete bug identified in its scoped formulas and deletion logic. These checks support implementation consistency, not scientific certification. The final files rebuild byte-for-byte from the parent, ranking and deletion records (`scripts/rebuild_final.py`).

