## 4. Experimental evidence and measurement comparability

`inputs/experimental_cases.json` preserves numerical values, actual or reconstructed mole fractions, units, source identifiers/URLs, diagnostic definitions, uncertainties and assumptions. Its 54 entries represent **50 distinct observed conditions plus four impurity-assumption variants**, not 54 independent experiments or studies. Observations were selected before candidate freezing and used only for final assessment. Source coverage and uncertainty types remain separate.

### Ignition measurements

There are 12 SFCPD targets from five studies: Petersen et al. (1996), Lifshitz et al. (1971), Shao et al. (2018), Qin et al. (2001), and Brown & Thomas (1999). Fourteen additional rows were transcribed directly from Shao's primary appendix, avoiding duplicate selected SFCPD rows. These cover H2, CH4, C2H4, C3H6, C3H8, CH4/C2H4 and C2H4/C3H6 blends at **1109–1905 K, 0.95–64 atm**, nominal phi 1 or 2 (1.00–2.02 from rounded reported compositions), and **91.2–99.85% Ar** by mole. Exact compositions are retained rather than inferred from fuel labels or phi alone.

Shao's primary author manuscript was read at `https://www.osti.gov/servlets/purl/1538299`. Its reflected shock tube has a 5-cm driven-section diameter; diagnostics are 1.1 cm from the end wall. OH* near 306 nm, IR absorption near 3.39 micrometres and pressure are used. Ignition onset is the maximum-signal-slope tangent extrapolated to the pre-ignition baseline. The reported post-shock pressure-rise nonideality was limited to 0.02/ms and judged negligible by the authors. We use a homogeneous adiabatic constant-volume reactor; boundary layers and individual facility histories are not modeled.

The pressure tangent is matched directly where pressure delay is tabulated. Where only OH* is available it is explicitly a **pressure-based proxy**, consistent with Shao's USC-Mech2 comparison because that mechanism lacks OH*. Ground-state OH is not OH* emission. Older SFCPD cases use their metadata-specified maximum OH growth or pressure growth diagnostics; their full primary apparatus/diagnostic methods were not recovered, so exact comparability remains uncertain. All diagnostic keys and proxy flags are retained per row.

Shao's typical stated uncertainty is 10%, rising to 15% below 100 microseconds; the confidence level is unspecified. SFCPD provides evaluated multiplicative factors, which are not original instrument-error bars. Four hydrogen targets also provide an **assumed 80 ppb atomic-H impurity** for modeling. This was not a measured component: clean compositions are the main comparison; four added-H variants are reported separately. Their mean absolute errors are approximately 28% across all models, versus about 33% for the four clean-H2 targets. Such sensitivity does not validate the impurity assumption.

### Flame-speed measurements

The 24 selected conditions span **298–400 K, 1–5 atm, phi approximately 0.63–1.40**, for mixtures of CH4, H2/CO, C2H2, C2H4, C2H6, C3H6 and C3H8. Twelve are SFCPD evaluated targets (four each CH4, H2/CO, C2H4), which may aggregate several studies. Six are Park et al. (2016) original propane/propene measurements. Six acetylene/ethane values are secondary transcriptions of reference 24 reproduced in Park Table 3; they are not new Park measurements.

Park's full text and methods were read at `https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5120407/`. Its counterflow twin flames use 1.4-cm nozzle diameter/separation, 298 K and 1 atm, PIV with silicone-oil droplets, upstream minimum velocity as reference speed, and nonlinear extrapolation to zero strain. Reported speed uncertainties are two-sigma; phi uncertainty is within 1%. The freely propagating adiabatic calculation approximates that extrapolated unstretched observable, not the finite-strain measured velocity. Radiation, heat loss and remaining stretch extrapolation error are not simulated.

Acetylene uses the reported **13% O2 / 87% N2 oxidizer**. Where Park states fuel/air and phi, air is reconstructed as O2:N2=1:3.76 and explicitly labeled a model assumption. The external flame comparisons use multicomponent transport plus Soret diffusion in the one-dimensional simulations. Keromnes et al. (2013) full-text methods informed H2/CO configuration/transport cautions; Bouvet and Bosschaart were available only at abstract/metadata level. No helium target was simulated because helium is absent from the supplied parent.

A researcher parser initially consumed the next percentage into a species name. That v1 dataset was rejected and archived **before simulation**. The corrected 92-row dataset was checked for species, composition sums and phi. Thirteen helium rows, five incomplete-mixture rows and six phi-inconsistent rows were flagged; those unsuitable records were excluded without guessing missing components. The selected cases passed explicit Cantera composition checks. Listed uncertainties were retained, including five dataset entries below 5%; no artificial uncertainty floor was imposed.

