## 5. Numerical and physical consistency

Cantera **3.2.0**, Python **3.12**, NumPy **2.5.3**, SciPy **1.18.1** and PyYAML **6.0.3** were used. Offline wheels and software versions are archived. Reactor tolerances are rtol=1e-9, atol=1e-18. The constant-volume diagnostic derivatives use Cantera rates:

`dT/dt = -sum(u_k omega_k)/(rho cv)`

`dP/dt = R [T sum(omega_k) + (rho/Wbar) dT/dt]`.

Pressure onset is `t_at_max_dPdt - (P-P0)/(dP/dt)`. Synthetic ignition uses maximum dT/dt. Highly diluted shock cases use a 0.01-K rise gate rather than the synthetic 200-K gate; the gate does not replace the chosen signal diagnostic. Metrics use every adaptive step. Final raw signal archives retain 200 distributed step indices plus exact diagnostic extrema; flame profiles retain the complete converged grid.

Synthetic flames use mixture-averaged transport and final refinement slope/curve 0.03/0.06, ratio 3, starting width 0.04 m, with solver-driven domain expansion. Eighteen earlier parent ignition checks at rtol=1e-11, atol=1e-20 and tau/500 maximum time step changed delay by at most **0.0515%**. Nine initial flame refinements changed speed by up to **1.593%**, motivating the common final finer grid. Doubling domain width in H2, CH4 and C2H2 tests changed speed by less than **0.00021%**. Multicomponent/Soret changed three parent examples by -10.96% (H2), -7.21% (syngas), and -1.15% (CH4), demonstrating that transport assumptions can exceed reduction error.

Final development convergence checks use four selected ignition cases, including worst-error propane cases, and five flames spanning H2, methane, acetylene and propane. Ignition tolerances tighten as above; flame slope/curve becomes 0.015/0.03. The table is a convergence check on selected cases, not a replacement for all-grid fidelity statistics.

